WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.250
Over the past 5 decades nuclear power has
prevented the release of 50 gigatonnes of

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:06.360
carbon dioxide.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:12.130
That’s equivalent to 2 years of total global
energy generation related emissions.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:14.960
[REF][1]
Nuclear Power is the most powerful tool at

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:21.120
our disposal to stop human driven climate
change in its tracks, yet powerful industrial

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.160
countries like Germany have turned their back
on this technology.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:28.260
Rapidly deactivating power plants prematurely.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:33.780
Nuclear power, despite its clear climate change
fighting potential, carries inherent risks

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:37.930
that have hampered political will to invest
in the technology.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:43.020
In order to thrive and help our planet overcome
its greatest challenge, nuclear Power needs

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.550
to evolve.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:49.320
This is the Future of Nuclear power.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:53.910
We can’t begin to address the solutions
of the future, without addressing the failures

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.550
of the past.

00:00:55.550 --> 00:01:00.920
Many of the most notorious nuclear meltdowns
were caused by errors in coolant systems.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:05.650
Take the three-mile island partial meltdown
incident of 1979.

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:10.100
The incident began with a mechanical failure
in the plant's cooling system.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:15.810
It all started when the steam generator stopped
receiving water due to a faulty clogged filter.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:20.270
The loss of water meant that the reactor's
energy didn't have anywhere to go, raising

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:22.130
the temperature of the reactor.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:26.220
As the pressure rose due to water being boiled,
a relief valve opened.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.220
[2][REF]

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:33.140
A valve designed to only open for 10 seconds,
but another fault resulted in the valve staying

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:34.140
open.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.400
Allowing the precious coolant inside the reactor
to escape.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.650
Pushing temperatures even higher.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:44.220
As the core temperature continued to rise,
the reactor operators received conflicting

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.509
information from the control room.

00:01:46.509 --> 00:01:52.100
The emergency water system that should have
provided extra water to the cores was blocked.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.710
Two days earlier, the plant was closed due
to maintenance, and the valves for the emergency

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:58.270
coolant were closed.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:03.090
Unfortunately, they were not reopened when
the reactor was restarted.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:07.510
The operator should have noticed that these
valves were closed but for reasons unknown,

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:10.789
he didn't see the warning lights in his panel.

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:15.230
One leading theory is that his stomach blocked
his view of the control panel.

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:21.530
Even then, The NRC, the regulatory body governing
nuclear safety, Had a rule that if the emergency

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:25.629
coolant valves were closed, the reactor must
be shut down.

00:02:25.629 --> 00:02:30.000
The Situation at Three Mile Island was in
clear violation of those rules.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.890
While the meltdown was triggered by a mechanical
failure, the situation was worsened by human

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:35.890
error.

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:40.519
The Three Mile Island incident serves as a
reminder of the critical importance of both

00:02:40.519 --> 00:02:46.849
robust mechanical safety systems and foolproof
controls in nuclear power plants.

00:02:46.849 --> 00:02:52.129
It highlights the need for attention to detail
in design, where even minor aspects like the

00:02:52.129 --> 00:02:57.379
positioning of alarms on control panels can
have far-reaching consequences, potentially

00:02:57.379 --> 00:03:00.239
leading to catastrophic meltdowns.

00:03:00.239 --> 00:03:05.239
To prevent such incidents, future nuclear
power plant designs must proactively address

00:03:05.239 --> 00:03:06.400
these issues.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:12.380
When the consequences of human error are nuclear
meltdowns, there is no room for human error.

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:13.380
[REF][3]

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:18.730
In Fukushima, an earthquake and its resulting
tsunami knocked out the plant's cooling systems.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:23.550
The cooling systems in Fukushima depended
on a separate energy source.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:29.440
The pumps that were needed to cool the reactors
lost power, resulting in a catastrophic meltdown.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.280
The plant did have a backup system, which
also failed.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:39.519
This need for external power to cool the reactors
was a key disadvantage of the design.

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:46.769
Fukushima also highlighted a weakness of current
nuclear reactors, the use of water as a coolant.

00:03:46.769 --> 00:03:51.860
Liquid water is a great conductor of heat
and serves as a fantastic cooling agent.

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:57.700
However, when control is lost this water can
lead to a high-pressure steam explosion, spreading

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:01.319
radioactive materials across vast distances.

00:04:01.319 --> 00:04:06.900
For this reason, many new reactor designs
seek to incorporate passive cooling systems,

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:13.760
which require no external power source, and
aim to replace water with safer cooling mediums.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:19.220
Before discussing these future designs, there
is another large problem facing Nuclear Energy.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:20.860
Its waste.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:25.200
Light-water reactors operate in what is called
an open fuel cycle.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:30.669
In this cycle, The uranium gets mined, processed,
enriched, used, and then stored.

00:04:30.669 --> 00:04:35.190
Leaving us with barrels of radioactive waste
that governments have been struggling to deal

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:36.190
with.

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:42.590
In the US, there has been a plan to create
deep geological storage facilities 660 meters

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:44.560
below Yucca Mountain.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:51.150
This has been the plan since the 1980s but
hasn't been put into action, so nuclear fuel

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:56.919
is still being stored in dangerous interim
storage facilities scattered around the country.

00:04:56.919 --> 00:05:02.720
As is the case with most nuclear energy projects
it has faced delays and a lack of funding.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:08.970
Obama, Trump, and Biden have all failed to
address this looming ecological threat.

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:12.650
Waste management issues have continually caused
political turmoil.

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:17.610
Shipments of nuclear waste from France to
Germany have been met with thousands of protestors,

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:23.060
a key driver of political pushback on the
development of nuclear energy.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:27.500
So is there an engineering solution to this
logistical concern?

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:31.040
Of Course, adequate long-term storage is possible.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:35.660
Sweden has approved large storage repositories
here in Frostmak. [4,5][REF][REF] Finland

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:39.460
has made similar arrangements to store its
Nuclear waste here.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:44.070
[4,6][REF] [REF]This Finnish deep geological
repository is expected to come online within

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:45.310
a year.

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:50.680
Deep geological repositories are sophisticated
engineered systems that employ multiple layers

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:55.310
of protective barriers to isolate the spent
fuel from the surrounding environment.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:56.310
[REF] [4]

00:05:56.310 --> 00:06:00.590
What if we could make use of this nuclear
waste, rather than locking it away for centuries

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:03.720
like a supernatural Zelda villain.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:06.340
Nuclear waste can be recycled.

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:11.500
During the early development of Nuclear power,
Uranium was thought to be a limited resource

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:17.250
so a lot of research was directed into creating
closed fuel cycles where the spent uranium

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:19.759
would be reprocessed and recycled.

00:06:19.759 --> 00:06:22.919
But the assumption of limited uranium was
wrong.

00:06:22.919 --> 00:06:25.810
Uranium is very common in the earth's crust.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:31.759
It’s much cheaper to mine, enrich, and process
uranium than it is to recycle nuclear waste

00:06:31.759 --> 00:06:33.360
into workable fuel.

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:36.520
Hence the adoption of an open fuel cycle.

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:42.190
This is the system currently operating on
most nuclear reactors with new waste piling

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:43.289
every minute.

00:06:43.289 --> 00:06:48.880
Recycling spent nuclear fuel is just a matter
of separating unused uranium from the fission

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:49.980
products.

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:55.020
This process is also not new, it was developed
in the 1950s and the basic steps are still

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:58.360
used today in France and in Japan.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:01.840
France for example, cools its nuclear waste
here.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:05.900
For three years it sits there before moving
to the reprocessing steps.

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:06.900
[REF] [7]

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:10.910
With the added cost, and the potential of
extracting plutonium for nuclear weapons,

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:15.260
the Carter administration banned US recycling
facilities in 1977.

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:16.449
[REF] [7]

00:07:16.449 --> 00:07:21.630
This recycled fuel can go back into regular
light water reactors, or it could go into

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:23.750
newer reactor designs.

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:29.280
Two common themes in new generation reactors
is passive cooling and higher thermal efficiencies

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:31.840
through high temperature coolants.

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:36.470
Light water reactors are limited to an operating
temperature of around 300 degrees celsius,

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:40.400
above this temperature the high pressure water
begins to boil.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:45.490
Thermal efficiency is how much electrical
energy we can produce from thermal energy,

00:07:45.490 --> 00:07:49.610
and in general, this efficiency gets better
with higher temperatures.

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:54.440
To increase efficiency, future nuclear reactors
can switch the coolant to something that can

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:56.509
handle higher temperatures.

00:07:56.509 --> 00:08:02.289
In the early 2000s, the US Department of Energy
decided to create the Gen IV international

00:08:02.289 --> 00:08:03.289
forum.

00:08:03.289 --> 00:08:08.470
It was composed of the best scientists around
the world to direct policy decisions and funding

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:10.930
for new reactor designs.

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:17.249
They chose a total of 6 new reactor technologies
and coined them Generation IV reactors.

00:08:17.249 --> 00:08:22.660
Instead of using water as a coolant, Gen IV
reactors can use gas, supercritical water,

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:28.470
molten salts, molten lead, or sodium to increase
the operating temperature of the reactor.

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:33.770
This increase in efficiency will result in
less nuclear waste per gigawatt generated,

00:08:33.770 --> 00:08:40.690
but these designs also aim to increase passive
cooling capabilities and limit nuclear proliferation

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:41.690
risks.

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:46.790
One of the most interesting prospects is the
Molten salt reactor or MSR.

00:08:46.790 --> 00:08:51.810
In these reactors, the coolant and the radioactive
fissile material are all combined.

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:57.691
The basic premise of these reactors was tested
and experimented with during the 1960s at

00:08:57.691 --> 00:09:00.260
the Oak Ridge National Laboratory

00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:06.610
In an MSR, the fuel consists of nuclear fuel,
such as uranium or thorium, dissolved in a

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:08.570
molten salt mixture.

00:09:08.570 --> 00:09:12.959
The salts typically used are fluorides or
chlorides, which have high melting points

00:09:12.959 --> 00:09:15.570
and good heat transfer properties.

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:20.320
The fuel salt circulates through the reactor
core, absorbing heat generated by nuclear

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:21.320
fission.

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:26.720
After it passes through the core, the fuel
salt transfers heat to a secondary salt coolant

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:27.720
loop.

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:32.190
The secondary salt, which does not contain
fissile material, absorbs the heat from the

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:35.750
fuel salt and carries it to a heat exchanger.

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:40.649
In the heat exchanger, the heat from the secondary
salt is transferred to a separate working

00:09:40.649 --> 00:09:45.160
fluid, typically water, which then powers
a steam generator.

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:49.080
Since the coolant that they use does not need
to be at high pressure like the water-cooled

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:53.950
reactors in Fukushima, salt reactors also
are not at risk of high-temperature steam

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:55.080
explosions.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:59.709
While light water reactors typically have
a thermal energy efficiency of around 30%,

00:09:59.709 --> 00:10:05.120
molten salt reactors have been theorized to
achieve efficiencies between 40% to 45% [REF]

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:06.120
[4].

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:10.570
Molten Salt Reactors also have the potential
for inline fuel processing, which means that

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:15.460
while the reactor is operating, the fuel salt
can be continuously reprocessed to remove

00:10:15.460 --> 00:10:17.940
fission products and add new fuel.

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:22.010
Meaning the machine can run continuously without
stopping for refueling.

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:26.730
At first glance, you'll notice that the system
needs not one, but two pumps.

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:32.730
However, these reactors can safely cool down
even when power is lost to these pumps.

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:38.899
The salts' chemical properties naturally inhibit
further nuclear fission as temperatures increase.

00:10:38.899 --> 00:10:43.810
The exact mechanisms of this depend on the
salt composition, but generally, as the molten

00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:46.399
salt gets hotter, it expands.

00:10:46.399 --> 00:10:51.700
This expansion decreases the volume of fuel
in the core of the reactors, and therefore

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:53.670
decreases the rate of nuclear fission.

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:54.670
[Ref] [Ref][8,9]

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:58.920
Meaning, the hotter it gets, the less fission
occurs, helping prevent nuclear meltdown.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:10:59.920
[REF][9]

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:03.880
A freeze plug is also located at the bottom
of the liquid salt pool.

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:07.600
The freeze plug is designed to melt at a particular
temperature.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:12.680
In the event of an uncontrolled rise in temperature
the plug will melt and allow the fuel and

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:16.570
molten salt to passively drain into cooled
dump tanks.

00:11:16.570 --> 00:11:17.570
[REF][9]

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:21.860
Molten Salt Reactors are some time away from
commercial readiness and that problem extends

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:27.920
to all other 4th generation reactors despite
significant support from proponents of these

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:29.410
technologies.

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:34.670
The 4th generation international forum originally
proposed that most of these technologies would

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:40.029
to be ready by 2020, but based on the fact
that the only footage available is from the

00:11:40.029 --> 00:11:43.480
1960s, that has not come to fruition.

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:48.240
Lack of funding, and competition from low
cost solar and wind, has held back development

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:50.890
of these fourth generation technologies.

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:57.190
This problem is only going to continue until
Nuclear energy addresses its largest problem.

00:11:57.190 --> 00:11:58.240
Cost.

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:04.240
Since 2003, MIT has been conducting studies
specifically aimed at guiding researchers

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:08.960
and policymakers towards a viable future for
nuclear energy.

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:13.900
In their latest report, they stated that the
utmost importance must be given to lowering

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:16.140
the cost of nuclear plants.[REF] [4].

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:20.440
And thus, the most promising ideas seek to
address these cost issues.

00:12:20.440 --> 00:12:25.400
While deep geological repositories, uranium
reprocessing, and Gen IV reactors had the

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:27.579
brunt of the work done during the 1900s.

00:12:27.579 --> 00:12:32.110
A new design concept started to emerge in
the early 2000s.

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:35.710
Small Modular Reactors, or SMRs for short.

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:41.120
The goal of this design is to miniaturize
reactors and convert them into small standardized

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:44.540
modules that can be fabricated in factories.

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:49.860
This standardization not only could decrease
costs but inherently increases their reliability

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:55.000
and safety as complexity is removed from the
manufacturing and assembly while decreasing

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:57.510
on-site construction costs.

00:12:57.510 --> 00:13:02.839
Small modular reactors work in much the same
way as regular nuclear plants, but with smaller

00:13:02.839 --> 00:13:07.020
individual reactors that can work in expandable
modules.

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:11.779
Gradually increasing the output of a power
plant with cheaper factory-made modules, rather

00:13:11.779 --> 00:13:17.320
than building one large custom-designed nuclear
power plant with 1000 Megawatts of electricity

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:18.320
capacity.

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:19.320
[REF][10]

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:22.720
Making the reactors smaller also comes with
the big benefit of passive safety.

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:27.699
A smaller reactor has more surface area for
heat transfer to occur in proportion to the

00:13:27.699 --> 00:13:30.529
volume of material that needs cooling.

00:13:30.529 --> 00:13:34.620
Meaning natural convection cycles are sufficient
to cool the reactor.

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:39.880
As the coolant is heated, it rises due to
its lower density, establishing a natural

00:13:39.880 --> 00:13:43.329
flow pattern that drives the cooling process.

00:13:43.329 --> 00:13:48.829
SMRs are not defined as one type of nuclear
reactor or one design, rather, they are a

00:13:48.829 --> 00:13:53.579
family of designs that takes advantage of
the miniaturization of the technology.

00:13:53.579 --> 00:13:57.029
Because of this, no one SMR is like the other.

00:13:57.029 --> 00:14:00.470
Some use Gen IV reactors, others use light
water reactors.

00:14:00.470 --> 00:14:05.870
Currently, over 70 commercial small modular
reactors are being developed.

00:14:05.870 --> 00:14:06.870
[REF][REF][11,12]

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:09.710
Nuscale may be the most promising of all these
companies.

00:14:09.710 --> 00:14:15.199
Their most recent 77MW Module is around 20
m tall and 4.5 m in diameter.

00:14:15.199 --> 00:14:19.940
[REF] [13] Each of these modules is lowered
into a water bath and set on top of seismic

00:14:19.940 --> 00:14:21.100
isolators.

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:24.519
This makes sure that any earthquakes do not
affect the reactor.

00:14:24.519 --> 00:14:28.579
[REF] [14] All of the components needed for
the nuclear reaction are fitted in one steel

00:14:28.579 --> 00:14:30.139
containment vessel.

00:14:30.139 --> 00:14:33.790
To create a larger plant, Multiple modules
are connected together.

00:14:33.790 --> 00:14:38.870
If power to the reactor is lost, control rods
fall automatically in a gravity driven mechanism,

00:14:38.870 --> 00:14:42.830
and the containment vessel seals its valves
to isolate itself.

00:14:42.830 --> 00:14:47.920
The water from the core is boiled off as steam
but stays inside the containment vessel.

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:51.740
Transferring heat with the outside cooling
pool, the steam condenses and pools at the

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:53.880
bottom of the reactor vessel.

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:59.000
Creating a natural circulation of cooling
water inside the steel containment vessel.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:03.240
Within seconds the energy and temperature
drop drastically and within a day the thermal

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:05.420
power drops by 90%.

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:08.139
Cooling off the last 10% over the course of
weeks.

00:15:08.139 --> 00:15:12.649
And while this all sounds great, we are nowhere
near commercial deployment.

00:15:12.649 --> 00:15:18.000
NuScale is by far the most ahead, but have
only managed to create a one-third scale model

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:19.300
of their power plant.

00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:20.300
[15][REF]

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:24.600
Other countries like Russia, China, France,
and South Korea have also invested in creating

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:28.940
SMR technologies but have struggled to find
utility customers.

00:15:28.940 --> 00:15:34.459
Without potential customers these startups
will always fail in a capitalist system.

00:15:34.459 --> 00:15:39.260
Even Nuslace has been dropped by some utility
clients as their prices seem to have exceeded

00:15:39.260 --> 00:15:40.260
expectations.

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:41.260
[REF] [16]

00:15:41.260 --> 00:15:45.839
This poses a massive challenge to companies
that rely on large-scale standardized factories

00:15:45.839 --> 00:15:48.279
to achieve their key market advantage.

00:15:48.279 --> 00:15:52.820
To build the factory they need customers,
but they can’t get customers without the

00:15:52.820 --> 00:15:53.850
factory.

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:58.589
Thousands of reactors will need to be built
before economies of scale can kick in, and

00:15:58.589 --> 00:16:04.320
in reality, the exact reason traditional nuclear
reactors produce so much electricity is to

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:07.370
benefit from the economies of scale.

00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:13.300
Building a 1000 MW reactor reduces the cost
per megawatt, and that’s THE most important

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:15.740
metric in the electricity market.

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:21.180
That’s how you get grid operators to buy
your electricity, by making it cheaper.

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:25.310
NuScales’ original price point was 55$/
MWh.

00:16:25.310 --> 00:16:31.360
However, due to inflation, rising steel costs,
development issues, and many delays the costs

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:37.970
are now estimated at 100$/ MWh. [17,18][REF][REF]
In comparison, onshore wind and solar can

00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:40.569
be as cheap as 30$/MWh [19].

00:16:40.569 --> 00:16:41.569
[REF].

00:16:41.569 --> 00:16:45.759
With the threat of climate change looming
over us, we need to ask ourselves.

00:16:45.759 --> 00:16:51.090
Can we rely on a capitalist system to fix
a problem driven by capitalism?

00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:56.470
There is a distinct possibility that these
technologies will never succeed without government

00:16:56.470 --> 00:16:57.470
funding.

00:16:57.470 --> 00:17:02.680
Energy generation is the foundation of every
major world economy, and it is in countries

00:17:02.680 --> 00:17:09.169
best interest to invest in these technologies
. Nuclear energy provides energy security.

00:17:09.169 --> 00:17:12.180
The US is not alone in trying to develop the
technology.

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:17.770
China has embarked on the construction of
a functional small modular reactor (SMR) project

00:17:17.770 --> 00:17:18.770
here.

00:17:18.770 --> 00:17:23.670
However, similar to many nuclear ventures,
costs have significantly escalated, with the

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:29.270
SMR reportedly being twice as expensive, in
terms of cost per kilowatt hour, compared

00:17:29.270 --> 00:17:32.530
to a traditional large-scale nuclear plant.

00:17:32.530 --> 00:17:33.530
[REF][20]

00:17:33.530 --> 00:17:38.600
Canada has formulated a 2020 SMR action plan
to help bring down cost, and have invested

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:41.170
millions of dollars into SMR start ups.

00:17:41.170 --> 00:17:42.170
[REF] [REF] [21,22]

00:17:42.170 --> 00:17:47.100
Developing these technologies will need vastly
more money than this, but it’s a step in

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:48.740
the right direction.

00:17:48.740 --> 00:17:53.549
Wind and solar are game changing technologies
that we could only have dreamed of being as

00:17:53.549 --> 00:17:59.980
cheap as they are today 2 decades ago, but
we need every tool at our disposal to fight

00:17:59.980 --> 00:18:01.289
climate change.

00:18:01.289 --> 00:18:05.990
Not just to decarbonize our energy generation,
but to start fighting the effects of climate

00:18:05.990 --> 00:18:10.049
change ,population growth and global industrialization.

00:18:10.049 --> 00:18:14.690
From increasing demand for air conditioning,
water scarcity driving the need for energy

00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:20.110
intensive water desalination, to last ditch
efforts to reverse climate change with carbon

00:18:20.110 --> 00:18:21.240
capture.

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:25.470
Energy is the cause and solution to our problems.

00:18:25.470 --> 00:18:31.270
The transition from fossil fuels, in my opinion,
is the most pressing issue facing humanity.

00:18:31.270 --> 00:18:33.960
This is an all hands on deck problem.

00:18:33.960 --> 00:18:38.440
We need our best minds working on it, and
I see it as part of my responsibility with

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:43.870
a platform as large as mine to inspire the
next generation of engineers to work on the

00:18:43.870 --> 00:18:45.150
solutions.

00:18:45.150 --> 00:18:49.850
Where I try to inspire, I see Brilliant as
the perfect partner for follow through.

00:18:49.850 --> 00:18:51.179
For education.

00:18:51.179 --> 00:18:56.410
The courses on Brilliant are perfect for getting
you prepared for engineering college, or for

00:18:56.410 --> 00:18:59.020
simply advancing your current career.

00:18:59.020 --> 00:19:01.870
From advanced mathematics courses to computer
science.

00:19:01.870 --> 00:19:07.610
They even have courses on the very foundation
of energy generation, electricity and magnetism.

00:19:07.610 --> 00:19:11.760
